What are all the possible target sums?

Since every element must be between 1 and limit:
- Minimum possible pair sum = 1 + 1 = 2
- Maximum possible pair sum = limit + limit = 2 * limit
Therefore, we can try every target sum from 2 to 2 * limit.

| Complexity      | Value        |
| --------------- | ------------ |
| Time            | O(n × limit) |
| Auxiliary Space | O(1)         |

class Solution {
public:
    int minMoves(vector<int>& nums, int limit) {
        int n = nums.size();
        int ans = INT_MAX;

        for(int tar = 2; tar <= 2*limit; tar++)
        {
            int mv = 0;

            for(int j = 0; j < n/2; j++)
            {
                int a = nums[j];
                int b = nums[n - 1 - j];

                if(a + b == tar)
                {
                    continue;
                }
                else if(tar - a >= 1 && tar - a <= limit)
                {
                    mv++;
                }
                else if(tar - b >= 1 && tar - b <= limit)
                {
                    mv++;
                }
                else{
                    mv+=2;
                }
            }
            ans = min(ans,mv);
        }
        return ans;
    }
};


//Optimal — Difference Array
Instead of checking every pair separately for every possible target sum, let's calculate the number of moves required for all possible target sums using a difference array.

Region 1: One Move Required

The minimum possible sum after changing at most one element is:

min(a, b) + 1

The maximum possible sum after changing at most one element is:

max(a, b) + limit

Therefore, the one-move range is:

[min(a, b) + 1, max(a, b) + limit]

Every target sum in this range requires at most one change.

Region 2: Zero Moves Required

The original sum is:

S = a + b

If the target sum equals the original sum:

moves = 0

Region 3: Two Moves Required

For target sums outside the one-move range, both elements need to be changed.

Therefore, for every pair:

- Initially, all target sums require 2 moves.
- In the one-move range, reduce the cost by 1.
- At the original sum, reduce the cost by another 1.

This is where the Difference Array comes in.

| Complexity      | Value        |
| --------------- | ------------ |
| Time            | O(n + limit) |
| Auxiliary Space | O(limit)     |

This is efficient enough for the given constraints.

class Solution {
public:
    int minMoves(vector<int>& nums, int limit) {

        int n = nums.size();

        vector<int> diff(2 * limit + 2, 0);

        for (int i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {

            int a = nums[i];
            int b = nums[n - 1 - i];

            // Initially, 2 moves for every target sum
            diff[2] += 2;
            diff[2 * limit + 1] -= 2;

            // Range where 1 move is sufficient
            int l = min(a, b) + 1;
            int r = max(a, b) + limit;

            diff[l]--;
            diff[r + 1]++;

            // Original sum requires 0 moves
            int sum = a + b;

            diff[sum]--;
            diff[sum + 1]++;
        }

        int moves = 0;
        int ans = INT_MAX;

        // Prefix sum
        for (int target = 2; target <= 2 * limit; target++) {

            moves += diff[target];

            ans = min(ans, moves);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};
